# Every base image this repository builds from, pinned by digest, and every
# toolchain it installs, pinned by version and source hash. PLAN-014 M2
# (RFCT-108); the decision is PLAN-014's decision 4.
#
# WHY SELF-BUILT AND NOT `golang:` / `rust:`. PLAN-014 decision 4 rejected the
# official language images "in favour of base+toolchain self-builds for total
# consistency". The official images are a moving floor nobody here chose: they
# pick their own Debian release, their own CA bundle, their own git, and they
# change all three on their own schedule. mos already builds its container
# engine and its updater from source for exactly this reason -- see
# os/podman/versions.env and os/update/rauc/versions.env -- and a build
# environment is no less a shipped decision than the binaries it produces.
# What this costs is written down in PLAN-014's risks: toolchain security
# updates now arrive by editing this file. That is the trade, taken knowingly.
#
# WHY DIGESTS AND NOT TAGS. `debian:trixie-slim` is a name upstream re-points
# whenever it rebuilds; `debian:trixie-slim@sha256:...` is the bytes. A tag
# means the answer to "what did this build run on" is a date, and every build
# before that date is unreproducible without one. The digest makes the base a
# value in this file, which is the same thing os/podman/versions.env does for
# the engine's version and for the same reason: it has to be answerable from
# the tree rather than from a build log.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE UPGRADE INTERFACE. Change a value here, run the build, read
# what it says. Nothing else in os/build-env/ carries an image name or a
# version.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MECHANICS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# SOURCED, NOT PARSED. os/build-env/build.sh reads this with `.`, and the
# Dockerfiles read a comment-stripped derivative of it (`images.lock`) the same
# way. So `${...}` on the right-hand side expands, and a value must be safe to
# eval. Keep to plain assignments.
#
# PIN THE INDEX DIGEST, NOT A PER-ARCHITECTURE MANIFEST DIGEST. `docker buildx
# imagetools inspect debian:trixie-slim` prints both: one `Digest:` for the
# whole multi-architecture index, then one per platform. The index digest is
# the one to record. A per-architecture digest builds fine on the host that
# recorded it and then refuses every cross build with a platform error, which
# is a failure this project would hit on its FIRST arm64 build and not before
# -- the single alpine pin already in the tree
# (board/cx3576/rootfs/alpine/Dockerfile) is an index digest too, so this
# convention is the one already in use rather than a new one.
#
# HOW TO BUMP A DIGEST: set the digest to the literal string PENDING -- keeping
# the `name:tag@` part, so the tag being resolved is still recorded -- and run
# `make build-env`. The build resolves the tag, prints the digest it found, and
# FAILS. Paste it in and run again. Same shape as bumping a hash in
# os/podman/versions.env, and for the same reason: recording a digest is then
# an act rather than a copy from a page nobody re-checked.
#
# PENDING IS A HARD FAILURE HERE, WITH NO ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE THAT SOFTENS IT.
# When M2a wrote this, it differed from the two files it took the pattern from:
# os/podman/Dockerfile and os/update/rauc/Dockerfile accepted PENDING with a
# warning unless MOS_PODMAN_STRICT / MOS_RAUC_STRICT was 1, on the reasoning
# that "an unhashed source can reach a developer and never a release". M2c
# checked that reasoning and it did not hold: NOTHING in this repository set
# either variable -- not .gitea/workflows/check.yml, not privileged.yml, not the
# Makefile, and not the two build.sh scripts, which passed through `:-0`. The
# release path and the developer path were the same warning, and
# os/podman/versions.env's own bump instructions had always said "The build
# prints the hash it computed and fails". So the knob was removed from both
# rather than described more carefully, and all three files now behave the way
# this one does.
#
# The reason it is right for a BASE IMAGE independently of that: an unpinned
# source at least fails a later hash check the moment one is recorded, but an
# unpinned base image fails nothing at all, ever, so a warning here floats for
# as long as nobody reads the log. One behaviour, in CI and on a laptop, and
# PENDING never survives a green build.
#
# WHAT A DIGEST PIN DOES NOT COVER, stated because the gap is easy to miss: it
# pins the base image's filesystem, NOT the apt archive that the build installs
# from on top of it. `apt-get install git` against a frozen debian:trixie-slim
# still resolves against live deb.debian.org and returns whatever trixie holds
# today. Closing that needs snapshot.debian.org (a dated archive URL, pinned
# here like anything else) and is not in RFCT-108's scope. Until then the floor
# minimums below are what stands between this build and a silent toolchain
# downgrade, which is why they are asserted rather than assumed, and the image
# records the versions it actually resolved (see os/build-env/base/Dockerfile).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NAMING, and the room left for the rest of M2
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   IMAGE_<distro>_<release>   an upstream base image, `name:tag@sha256:...`
#   <IMAGE>_FLOOR_<tool>_MIN   the minimum version a mos-build-<image> asserts
#   <IMAGE>_<tool>_VERSION     a toolchain tarball's version
#   <IMAGE>_<tool>_SHA256      that tarball's source hash
#
# The prefix before the first underscore is the CONSUMER, and that is
# load-bearing rather than decorative: os/build-env/build.sh gives each image a
# lock file filtered to its own prefix, so adding a pin for one image does not
# invalidate the cached layers of the others. os/podman/Dockerfile measured
# what the alternative costs -- one shared dependency list meant a one-package
# edit recompiled five unrelated components, about 42 minutes under emulation
# -- and the same arithmetic applies to a shared lock file.
#
# So M2b adds GO_/RUST_/C_ blocks and M2c adds IMAGE_ lines, and neither has to
# reshape anything above.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Base images
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The floor for everything mos builds itself. trixie is not a preference here:
# os/rootfs/stages/10-base.Dockerfile assembles the device root from trixie, so a builder
# on any other release would link against a different glibc, libseccomp and
# libsubid than the one the binaries run on -- os/podman/Dockerfile records
# that constraint and the ten build iterations that established it.
#
# Recorded 2026-08-25. Upstream's own metadata dates this build 2026-08-24.
IMAGE_DEBIAN_TRIXIE=debian:trixie-slim@sha256:d7e12182ce18b85b93007c1dedf31f2d29e01ccf3182cc4017c709b6259bc132

# The pack stage of os/rootfs/stages/90-pack.Dockerfile, and since R6 the three containers
# in os/tests/repart-loader-test.sh. It is one release behind the rootfs on
# purpose and the reason is recorded at that stage: the packer runs
# squashfs-tools, cryptsetup and libcap2-bin over an already assembled root, and
# moving it forward is a change to the packing tools, not to what is packed.
# Pinned here so that "one release behind" is a value in this file rather than a
# tag that follows bookworm's own point releases.
#
# THIS COMMENT USED TO READ "and NOTHING ELSE", and R6 made that false rather
# than found it so. Those three containers named `debian:bookworm-slim`
# literally and were routed to this key because it is the key for that image --
# IMAGE_ keys in this file are one per upstream reference, not one per consumer,
# which is why IMAGE_DEBIAN_TRIXIE already has six. Recorded here because the
# alternative reading is available and wrong: this is NOT a second decision that
# the repart test should track the packer. If the packer moves to trixie, that
# is a change to the packing tools and the test's bases have to be looked at
# rather than carried along. This is the only edit R6 made to an entry that
# already existed, and it changed prose and not a pin.
#
# Recorded 2026-08-25 through the PENDING flow above.
IMAGE_DEBIAN_BOOKWORM=debian:bookworm-slim@sha256:88200866dfff7ea7f5cbcb6ec7c8a701889efe6fe859fe64d6990e4b07ea4171

# The BSP builders (board/cx3576/{uboot,kernel}/Dockerfile,
# board/cx3576/Dockerfile.alpine, the pack stage of
# board/cx3576/rootfs/alpine/Dockerfile) and os/tests/handshake-test.
#
# UBUNTU AND NOT TRIXIE, AND NOT RESTRUCTURED. RFCT-108 draws that boundary
# explicitly: the BSP builders "get digest pins only". They cross-compile a
# vendor kernel and a vendor U-Boot with gcc-aarch64-linux-gnu,
# python3-pyelftools, swig and device-tree-compiler at versions those vendor
# trees were tested against; moving them onto mos-build-c would be a rebuild of
# a BSP under a toolchain nobody has booted on the hardware, which is a
# different milestone from pinning what they already run on. What this key buys
# is the one property they lack today: `ubuntu:24.04` is a tag Canonical
# repoints on every point release, so the answer to "which builder produced this
# u-boot.itb" was a date.
#
# Recorded 2026-08-25 through the PENDING flow above.
IMAGE_UBUNTU_2404=ubuntu:24.04@sha256:33ceb71981b602c1a7443a53469e4dba065f7503eab3078a2d7a57a2ab987517

# The rootfs stage of board/cx3576/rootfs/alpine/Dockerfile -- the writable
# board-debug system, which is Alpine and not Debian for reasons that predate
# this plan (board/cx3576/rootfs/alpine/README.md).
#
# THIS PIN IS NOT NEW; IT IS RELOCATED. It was already an index digest, written
# as a default on `ARG ALPINE_IMAGE` in that Dockerfile, and M2a cited it as the
# precedent for pinning index digests rather than per-architecture ones. Moving
# it here changes nothing about which bytes are used and one thing about the
# file: a pin that lives beside its own FROM is a pin only that FROM's author
# reads, and the point of this file is that there is one place to answer "what
# does this repository build on".
#
# THE DIGEST IS CARRIED OVER VERBATIM, not re-resolved. Re-resolving would have
# moved the board-debug rootfs to whatever alpine:3.24.1 serves today as a side
# effect of a refactor -- an unannounced base change, which is the exact thing
# this file exists to make impossible. Bumping it is still one PENDING away.
IMAGE_ALPINE_3_24_1=alpine:3.24.1@sha256:28bd5fe8b56d1bd048e5babf5b10710ebe0bae67db86916198a6eec434943f8b

# The ASSEMBLY and VERIFICATION containers for cx3576, and the loop-device
# probe in .gitea/workflows/privileged.yml. os/mkimage-v2.sh writes the GPT,
# the ESP and the filesystems of the shipped image from inside this base;
# os/verify-image-v2.sh reads them back out of it.
#
# A CONTAINER THAT WRITES A RELEASE IMAGE IS A BUILD ENVIRONMENT. That is the
# whole reason this key exists, and it is worth writing down because the
# distinction is easy to lose: mos-build-c is pinned because it COMPILES what
# ships, and this is pinned because it PARTITIONS and FORMATS what ships. The
# second is not a lesser claim on reproducibility than the first -- a base that
# moved under os/mkimage-v2.sh changes sgdisk, mkfs.vfat, mcopy and mke2fs all
# at once, which is every tool that decides the on-disk bytes of a device
# image. PLAN-014 decision 4 covers it in principle; it was outside RFCT-108's
# Scope sentence only because that sentence happened to name Dockerfile FROM
# lines, and the M2 gate found this by re-deriving the list rather than reading
# the sentence.
#
# WHY 3.21 AND NOT IMAGE_ALPINE_3_24_1 ABOVE. These are two different decisions
# that happen to share a distro. 3.24.1 is the board-debug rootfs -- a system
# that BOOTS -- and 3.21 is a toolbox that runs sgdisk and mke2fs and exits.
# Collapsing them onto one key would mean bumping the debug rootfs whenever the
# assembler wanted a newer mtools, and the assembler's e2fsprogs floor
# (mke2fs >= 1.47, for `-O ^orphan_file` and `-E hash_seed`) is the constraint
# that actually chose 3.21. Held at the version the tree already ran on, for the
# same reason the 3.24.1 digest was carried over verbatim rather than
# re-resolved: a refactor must not be how the assembler's toolset moves.
#
# Recorded 2026-08-25 through the PENDING flow above. It is an OCI index over
# amd64, arm64, arm, 386, ppc64le, riscv64 and s390x -- checked, because the
# convention at the top of this file is the index digest and a per-architecture
# one would build here and refuse every cross build. Upstream's own metadata
# labels this index 3.21.7, built 2026-04-16.
IMAGE_ALPINE_3_21=alpine:3.21@sha256:48b0309ca019d89d40f670aa1bc06e426dc0931948452e8491e3d65087abc07d

# WHO READS THIS KEY: os/verify/run.sh, which resolves it with
# `os/build-env/from.sh --ref IMAGE_BUN_1` and runs bun in it on any host that
# has no bun binary. That is RFCT-109's tool-less-host clause, and since M3b it
# is a regression-closer rather than a nicety -- `make os-layout-lint` used to
# run on bare bash and now needs bun like the suite does, so this digest is what
# keeps a board definition checkable on a host with only docker. It is also what
# CI runs: .gitea/workflows/check.yml installs no bun, so the runner takes this
# route and the pin is exercised on every push rather than only when someone
# happens to lack bun.
#
# The paragraph this replaced said nothing read the key yet, which was true when
# R6 recorded it ahead of its consumer -- deliberately, because it is a RESOLVED
# digest rather than a PENDING, confirmed to be an OCI index over amd64 and
# arm64 rather than a per-architecture manifest. M3c inherited a value rather
# than a task, and this is the note that it is now spent.
#
# THE SECOND CONSUMER, WHICH IS STILL NOT WIRED. test/apid-api/run.sh -- the API
# conformance harness, which runs its TypeScript client against a booted guest
# -- takes its image from MOS_APID_BUN_IMAGE with `oven/bun:1` as the default.
# R6 wired that default to this key and the wiring was REVERTED, because
# PLAN-014's scope sentence names
# `test/apid-api` as out of scope for this campaign and a finding does not
# widen a boundary the plan drew. The finding was escalated to L1 instead. Read
# the paragraph below as the case attached to it, not as a description of
# something this tree currently does.
#
# THE LOOSEST FLOAT THE R6 SWEEP FOUND, and it was not on the M2 gate's list.
# The gate greped for the distro names this tree builds on -- alpine, debian,
# ubuntu -- so an image named `oven/bun` was invisible to it, and what was
# invisible was `oven/bun:1`: not a patch tag, not even a minor one, but a
# MAJOR-version tag that upstream repoints onto every 1.x release. The rest of
# the inventory could drift by a rebuild; this one could drift by a feature
# release, and the harness it runs decides whether apid's API is judged
# conformant. That is the case for closing it; whose it is to close is a
# separate question, and the answer today is not this campaign's.
#
# NOT A DISTRO, WHICH THE KEY NAME SHOULD NOT HIDE. Every other IMAGE_ key here
# is <distro>_<release>; this is <vendor product>_<major>. The naming rule the
# header states is IMAGE_<distro>_<release>, and this is the first key that
# does not fit it -- recorded as a stretch of the convention rather than
# silently, so the next reader does not take it as a distro they have not heard
# of.
#
# Recorded 2026-08-25 through the PENDING flow above.
IMAGE_BUN_1=oven/bun:1@sha256:5ff609364c049b54eb0ff560ec96319729a972078ef2c755d758f0c6ef89c2d6

# THE DOCKERFILE FRONTEND -- the `# syntax=` line at the top of all twelve
# Dockerfiles in this tree. Not a FROM, and the thirteenth kind of unpinned
# reference the R6 sweep had to rule on.
#
# WHAT IT IS. BuildKit reads `# syntax=<image>` before it reads anything else
# and hands the whole Dockerfile to THAT image to parse and lower into a build
# graph. It is remote code, fetched at build time, with the ability to decide
# what every instruction in every Dockerfile here means. `docker/dockerfile:1`
# is a major-version channel upstream repoints on every 1.x release.
#
# WHY IT IS PINNED. Not for reproducibility of the bytes: the frontend emits
# none: it translates a Dockerfile, and a frontend release that changed what
# `apt-get install` produced would be a BuildKit bug rather than drift. The
# reason is the other one. This repository signs update bundles that devices
# install, and the frontend is the one remote component that runs with the
# power to rewrite the build that produces them. PLAN-014 decision 4 says a
# build environment becomes a decision recorded in the tree; a parser with that
# reach is inside that sentence however narrowly it is read.
#
# WHY IT IS THE ONE PIN THAT IS ALSO WRITTEN OUT AT ITS TWELVE CALL SITES, and
# why that does not make it a second table. `# syntax=` is consumed before any
# ARG exists, so it cannot take a --build-arg and os/build-env/from.sh cannot
# reach it -- there is no mechanism by which twelve Dockerfiles could read this
# value at build time. The twelve literals are therefore unavoidable, and what
# makes them safe is that os/build-env/build.sh CHECKS them against this key on
# every run and refuses a tree where any of them has drifted. The rule M2b set
# was that a second table must not be able to disagree with the first; this one
# cannot, because disagreeing is a hard failure of `make build-env`.
#
# PINNING IT CHANGED NO BUILD ON THE DAY IT WAS RECORDED: this digest is what
# `docker/dockerfile:1` already resolved to, so the frontend every build here
# was already getting is the frontend now named. Bumping it is one PENDING and
# twelve one-line edits that build.sh will insist on.
#
# Recorded 2026-08-25 through the PENDING flow above. An OCI index over twelve
# architectures.
IMAGE_DOCKERFILE_FRONTEND=docker/dockerfile:1@sha256:ecfaec9ed6d810b56388c508f4121597bfbba70d41a6dfeee4d8cad5f295fc32

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# mos-build-base: the common floor
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The five packages every builder needs regardless of language, and nothing
# else. Per-language apt lists stay in their own Dockerfiles -- see the
# 42-minute measurement cited above; that is the same decision, applied to apt
# rather than to lock files.
#
# These are MINIMUMS, not pins, because the digest above does not pin the apt
# archive (see WHAT A DIGEST PIN DOES NOT COVER). What they catch is the
# direction that matters: a floor tool that went missing, or went BACKWARDS,
# which is what a base swap or a slimmed package looks like from inside the
# build. A trixie point release moving git forward passes, as it should.
#
# The values are trixie's, measured 2026-08-25 against the digest above:
# git 2.47.3, file 5.46, binutils 2.44, xz 5.8.1. Raise one only when trixie
# has actually moved, or the assertion stops meaning anything.
BASE_FLOOR_CODENAME=trixie
BASE_FLOOR_GIT_MIN=2.47
BASE_FLOOR_FILE_MIN=5.46
BASE_FLOOR_BINUTILS_MIN=2.44
BASE_FLOOR_XZ_MIN=5.8

# M2b adds the toolchain blocks -- GO_VERSION/GO_SHA256_<arch>,
# RUST_VERSION/RUST_SHA256_<arch>, and whatever C_ floor mos-build-c asserts --
# each pinned to a tarball hash the way os/podman/versions.env pins a source
# tree, and each asserted inside its own image the way BASE_FLOOR_* is asserted
# inside this one.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The images THIS repository builds, named for the FROM lines that consume them
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Not an IMAGE_ key, and that is a statement rather than a naming slip. IMAGE_
# means "an upstream reference, pinned by digest, and os/build-env/build.sh
# refuses it if it is not"; this is an image THIS repository built, moments
# earlier in the same run, from IMAGE_DEBIAN_TRIXIE above. It has no digest that
# could be recorded here -- mos-build-base's id is not reproducible across a
# rebuild, because the apt layer is not and because buildx attaches a fresh
# provenance attestation even when every layer is CACHED (measured: two runs of
# an entirely cached mos-build-base produced two different ids). A digest
# written here would be a value that goes stale on the next rebuild and teaches
# whoever hits it to stop believing this file.
#
# What pins the child images is therefore the parent's OWN pin, one level up:
# mos-build-base is built from a digest, asserts its floor, and records what it
# resolved in /etc/mos-build/base.env. The chain is pinned at its root, which is
# the only place a chain can be pinned.
#
# The LOCAL_ prefix is also load-bearing for the lock filter: no image row lists
# it, so these keys reach no image's images.lock and cannot invalidate anyone's
# layers. They travel as a --build-arg and nothing else.
LOCAL_MOS_BUILD_BASE=localhost/mos-build-base

# M2c's switchover targets. mos-build-base is the FROM of the other three, and
# these four are the FROM of every component build in the tree: os/podman's src,
# c-build, rust-build, go-build and verify stages, os/update/rauc's src and build
# stages, and mosd/hack/build-target.sh's cross build.
#
# WHY THEY ARE KEYS AND NOT LITERALS IN THOSE DOCKERFILES. The same reason every
# IMAGE_ line above is a key: os/build-env/from.sh is the one place that answers
# "what does this build run on", and a FROM that names an image directly is a
# second answer nobody has to keep in step. It also means renaming the family --
# or building a second family side by side under another prefix -- is an edit to
# this file rather than a sweep through eight Dockerfiles.
#
# WHAT THESE PIN, EXACTLY, stated because a tag in a pin file reads like a
# contradiction: nothing, on their own. They are local tags, and their bytes are
# whatever `make build-env` last wrote -- which is why os/build-env/from.sh
# refuses one that is not in the local image store, rather than letting docker
# report it as a failed registry pull of a host called `localhost`. What makes
# the CHAIN pinned is one level up: mos-build-base is built from
# IMAGE_DEBIAN_TRIXIE, asserts its floor, and records what it resolved. A
# component build standing on these tags is pinned exactly as far as the run of
# `make build-env` that produced them was, and no further.
LOCAL_MOS_BUILD_C=localhost/mos-build-c
LOCAL_MOS_BUILD_GO=localhost/mos-build-go
LOCAL_MOS_BUILD_RUST=localhost/mos-build-rust

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# mos-build-c: the C/C++ toolchain
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# FLOORS, not exact versions, and for the same reason BASE_FLOOR_* are floors:
# every one of these comes from `apt-get install` against live deb.debian.org,
# which the digest above does not pin (see WHAT A DIGEST PIN DOES NOT COVER).
# An exact match would go red on the next trixie point release. The Go and Rust
# blocks below are the opposite case -- a tarball pinned by sha256 IS exact, and
# is asserted exactly -- and the difference between the two is not a matter of
# taste: it is whether the thing being asserted is pinned or merely requested.
#
# The values are trixie's, measured 2026-08-25 against the digest above with
# mos-build-c's own apt list: gcc 14.2.0, make 4.4.1, cmake 3.31.6, pkgconf
# 1.8.1, ccache 4.11.2. Raise one only when trixie has actually moved.
#
# autoconf, automake, libtoolize and python3 are asserted by PRESENCE and carry
# no floor. They are build-time drivers whose output is the project's own
# configure script, not libraries anything links against, and a floor on a
# driver is a number nobody can justify raising -- an assertion that cannot be
# maintained honestly is one that gets widened until it means nothing. What
# breaks if they vanish is the build, immediately and by name, which is what
# the presence check is for. Note libtoolize and not libtool: the `libtool`
# package installs libtoolize, and the `libtool` command itself is generated
# per-project by configure, so a check for it fails on a correct image.
C_FLOOR_GCC_MIN=14.2
C_FLOOR_MAKE_MIN=4.4
C_FLOOR_CMAKE_MIN=3.31
C_FLOOR_PKGCONF_MIN=1.8
C_FLOOR_CCACHE_MIN=4.11

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# mos-build-go: the Go toolchain, from the upstream tarball
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHY 1.26.7 AND NOT 1.27.0. Go supports the two most recent major lines, so
# 1.26.x and 1.27.x are both current; 1.26.7 is the latest patch of the mature
# one and 1.27.0 is days old at the time of writing. A build environment is the
# wrong place to be first. Note that this is still a BUMP relative to what the
# tree does today -- os/podman/Dockerfile's `GO_IMAGE=golang:1.25-trixie` is a
# line Go no longer supports, so M2c's switchover changes the toolchain as well
# as the image it comes from, and podman's build has to be re-proven under it.
# Deliberate: a builder image whose compiler gets no security fixes is not a
# build environment anyone chose either.
#
# WHY THE URL LIVES HERE. images.env is sourced, and so is the comment-stripped
# lock derived from it, so ${GO_VERSION} expands in both. The version therefore
# appears exactly once in the tree, and a bump is one edit rather than one edit
# plus two URLs somebody has to notice. GO_VERSION must stay above the URLs that
# read it -- a sourced file is ordered.
#
# THE HASHES WERE RESOLVED BY os/build-env/build.sh AND THEN CHECKED AGAINST
# UPSTREAM'S OWN PUBLISHED CHECKSUMS (go.dev/dl's release metadata, and for the
# Rust block below static.rust-lang.org's channel manifest). Both directions
# matter: the resolver proves the bytes this repository can actually fetch, and
# the published checksum proves those bytes are the ones upstream signed off on.
# Recording only one of the two would be recording that the download worked.
#
# NOTHING PINS CROSS-COMPILATION FOR GO, and that is a property of Go rather
# than an omission: the stock tarball cross-compiles to every GOOS/GOARCH it
# supports with no extra component and no cross linker, which is why there is no
# GO_STD_* block here and there is a RUST_STD_* block below. mos-build-go
# asserts that rather than assuming it.
GO_VERSION=1.26.7
GO_URL_AMD64=https://go.dev/dl/go${GO_VERSION}.linux-amd64.tar.gz
GO_URL_ARM64=https://go.dev/dl/go${GO_VERSION}.linux-arm64.tar.gz
GO_SHA256_AMD64=ffb5f8de10c62550dfddab66b36b57030721e0a44a3218e9e1181d7b59f121ca
GO_SHA256_ARM64=5a4ec883379d51ee9ce1040d5e87f8d35e20387574dd8c947feb01eabc3c1b37

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# mos-build-rust: the Rust toolchain, from the upstream standalone installer
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHY THE CURRENT STABLE, where Go took the mature line. The two projects have
# different support policies and the pin should follow the policy rather than a
# house style: Go supports two major lines at once, so there is a mature one to
# choose; Rust fixes only the current stable, so anything older is a line that
# receives nothing. mosd/Cargo.toml declares rust-version = 1.96 and edition
# 2024, which 1.98.0 satisfies.
#
# THE TRIPLE IS A KEY because it is used twice -- once to build the URL and once
# to name the rust-std component install.sh has to be asked for. Written out
# twice it is a thing that can disagree with itself.
#
# THE URLS ARE THE UNDATED PERMALINKS, not the dated dist/<date>/ paths the
# channel manifest publishes. Both serve identical bytes -- checked, the sha256s
# below match the manifest's xz_hash entries exactly -- and the undated form is
# derivable from RUST_VERSION, which is what keeps the version a single value in
# this file instead of a value plus a release date nobody can reconstruct.
#
# RUST_STD_* IS THE CROSS-COMPILATION PIN. The standalone `rust-` tarball ships
# std for its own host triple only, so a mos-build-rust built from it can build
# for exactly one target -- and the one target this project actually ships to is
# the OTHER one (mosd/hack/build-aarch64.sh, and mosd/.cargo/config.toml's
# aarch64 linker entry, are both about cross-building an aarch64 device binary
# from an amd64 host). The cross std is a separate upstream tarball, pinned here
# the same way, and the cross LINKER is an apt package asserted in the image.
# RFCT-108 puts mosd/hack/build-target.sh inside this image in M2c; an image
# that could not cross-compile would be a handoff that cannot be taken.
RUST_VERSION=1.98.0
RUST_TRIPLE_AMD64=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu
RUST_TRIPLE_ARM64=aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu
RUST_URL_AMD64=https://static.rust-lang.org/dist/rust-${RUST_VERSION}-${RUST_TRIPLE_AMD64}.tar.xz
RUST_URL_ARM64=https://static.rust-lang.org/dist/rust-${RUST_VERSION}-${RUST_TRIPLE_ARM64}.tar.xz
RUST_SHA256_AMD64=ed8ee2df70909c88cbaf87a6cfa3920dac00b537de12a6abe6906641e0f5952f
RUST_SHA256_ARM64=ac9283184301aeed06ecc9f5aa4c1be7041e18a1b197b6cb6c5d162d98f566da
RUST_STD_URL_AMD64=https://static.rust-lang.org/dist/rust-std-${RUST_VERSION}-${RUST_TRIPLE_AMD64}.tar.xz
RUST_STD_URL_ARM64=https://static.rust-lang.org/dist/rust-std-${RUST_VERSION}-${RUST_TRIPLE_ARM64}.tar.xz
RUST_STD_SHA256_AMD64=f5022e6c95a5ad23cca2513dc8281200f585fa188de6370aa37b128a43f876a3
RUST_STD_SHA256_ARM64=a36f7ac98af20ef0ba6368aace0345efabbebaef7962eba99f47190fd256162d
